    answer: 'ROC-AUC plots TPR against FPR across thresholds and equals the probability a random positive is scored above a random negative (Mann-Whitney U). It is invariant to class prevalence because both axes are within-class rates. PR-AUC plots precision against recall; precision depends on the ratio of true positives to predicted positives, so it is sensitive to prevalence. When positives are rare — fraud, disease, click-through on rare events at, say, 1% prevalence — ROC-AUC can look excellent (0.95) while precision at useful recall is terrible, because even a tiny FPR translates to a flood of false positives given the huge negative base. PR-AUC exposes that. So: use ROC-AUC when classes are roughly balanced or when you care about ranking quality independent of prevalence; use PR-AUC when the positive class is rare and the cost of the problem lives in the positive predictions. A concrete tell: at 1% prevalence, an FPR of 5% with 100% recall gives precision around 1/(1+5) ≈ 17% — ROC hides that, PR shows it.',
