    answer: 'CUPED (Controlled experiment Using Pre-Experiment Data) reduces variance by using a pre-experiment covariate that is correlated with the metric but unaffected by the treatment. Instead of comparing the raw metric Y, you compare an adjusted metric Y_cuped = Y − θ(X − E[X]), where X is the pre-period value of the same (or a correlated) metric and θ = Cov(Y, X)/Var(X) is the variance-minimizing coefficient. Because X is measured before treatment, subtracting it does not bias the treatment effect (E[X] is the same in both arms in expectation), but it strips out the pre-existing user-level variation that is just noise for our purposes. The variance reduction is 1 − ρ², where ρ is the correlation between Y and X — so if the pre-period metric correlates 0.7 with the in-experiment metric, you cut variance by ~49%, roughly halving the sample size needed or letting you detect a ~30% smaller effect. Assumptions: X must be pre-treatment (or otherwise unaffected by assignment) — using an in-experiment covariate reintroduces bias; the relationship should be roughly linear for the θ adjustment; and it helps most for high-variance, high-autocorrelation metrics (revenue per user, engagement) and little for metrics with weak pre-period signal (a brand-new feature nobody used before). Failure modes: new users with no pre-period data (needs a fallback), covariate that is actually post-randomization, and over-fitting θ on small samples.',
