    answer: 'Frequentist testing fixes a null hypothesis, controls the Type-I error rate across hypothetical repetitions, and reports a p-value and confidence interval — the "95% CI" is a statement about the procedure, not the probability the effect is in the interval. It requires a fixed sample size (or explicit sequential corrections) because peeking inflates false positives. Bayesian testing puts a prior on the effect, updates to a posterior, and reports directly interpretable quantities: P(treatment > control), the posterior distribution of the lift, and expected loss of shipping the wrong arm. Practical reasons to go Bayesian: (1) decision-oriented outputs — "92% probability B beats A, expected loss of shipping B is $X" maps to a business call more naturally than a p-value; (2) it handles optional stopping more gracefully via expected-loss thresholds (though it is not immune to bias from stopping rules — a common misconception); (3) priors let you incorporate historical knowledge, useful for low-traffic experiments where frequentist tests are underpowered; (4) it naturally supports multi-armed and continuous-monitoring setups (Thompson sampling). Reasons to stay frequentist: regulatory/organizational expectation of guaranteed error control, prior-sensitivity concerns, and simpler communication when stakeholders expect p-values. In practice I choose based on the decision: fixed-horizon guaranteed-error-rate launches → frequentist (often with sequential testing for early stopping); low-traffic, decision-loss-driven, or bandit-style allocation → Bayesian. Both need the same care about interference, SRM, and metric selection.',
