    answer: 'AUC only measures ranking — it is invariant to any monotonic transform of scores — so a model can rank perfectly yet output probabilities that are systematically off. GBDTs are prone to this: the trees optimize a loss that produces good discrimination, but boosting tends to push scores toward the extremes (over-confident), and if you tuned for AUC or used aggressive regularization / early stopping, the raw scores need not be well-calibrated. Class imbalance and subsampling make it worse. Detect it with a reliability diagram (bin by predicted prob, plot mean predicted vs. observed frequency) and summarize with Expected Calibration Error or Brier score (Brier decomposes into calibration + refinement). Fix by post-hoc calibration on a held-out set the model did not train on: Platt scaling (fit a logistic on the scores — good when the miscalibration is a smooth sigmoid distortion and data is limited) or isotonic regression (non-parametric monotonic fit — more flexible, corrects arbitrary monotonic distortion but needs more data and can overfit on small sets). If I retrain, I make sure the objective is a proper scoring rule (log-loss) rather than something that only rewards ranking. Important nuance: calibration must be fit and validated on data separate from both training and the calibration fit, and re-checked after any distribution shift, because calibration drifts even when discrimination holds.',
