    answer: 'Both are tree ensembles but combine trees oppositely. Random forests are bagging: many deep, low-bias/high-variance trees trained on bootstrap samples with random feature subsets, then averaged — averaging decorrelated trees drives down variance, so RFs mainly attack variance and are hard to overfit by adding trees. Gradient boosting builds shallow trees sequentially, each fitting the residual/gradient of the current ensemble — it mainly attacks bias, so it can reach lower error but will overfit if you add too many trees or use too high a learning rate, which is why it needs early stopping and careful regularization. Practical tradeoffs: RFs are more robust out-of-the-box, need little tuning, parallelize trivially (trees are independent), and give a free OOB error estimate — good when I want a quick strong baseline, robustness, or when tuning budget is limited. GBDTs (XGBoost/LightGBM/CatBoost) usually win the accuracy race on tabular data and are the default for leaderboard-style performance, but they are sequential, more tuning-sensitive, and easier to overfit. For very noisy labels or when I fear overfitting, RF is safer; when I want maximal performance and can invest in tuning + early stopping, GBDT. I would also note that "add more trees" is safe for RF but dangerous for boosting — a common confusion.',
