    answer: 'Both add a penalty on coefficient magnitude to the loss to shrink weights and reduce variance, but the geometry differs. L2 (ridge) penalizes the sum of squared weights; its gradient is proportional to the weight, so it shrinks large weights hard and small weights gently, never driving them exactly to zero — it distributes shrinkage smoothly and handles correlated features by splitting weight among them. L1 (lasso) penalizes the sum of absolute weights; its subgradient is constant (sign of the weight), a fixed pull toward zero regardless of magnitude, so small weights get zeroed out — that produces exact sparsity and thus feature selection. Geometrically, the L1 constraint region is a diamond with corners on the axes, and the loss contours tend to touch it at a corner where some coordinates are exactly zero, whereas the L2 ball is round with no corners. Choose L1 when you want a sparse, interpretable model or automatic feature selection, especially with many irrelevant features; but with a group of correlated predictors lasso arbitrarily keeps one and drops the rest, which can be unstable. Choose L2 when features are correlated and you want to keep them all with shrunk, stable coefficients, or when you just want variance reduction without selection. Elastic Net combines both (L1 for sparsity + L2 for grouping stability) and is often the pragmatic default when p is large and features are correlated. From a Bayesian view, L2 is a Gaussian prior on weights and L1 is a Laplace prior.',
