    answer: 'The cleanest and most numerically robust approach uses the Mann-Whitney U interpretation: AUC equals the probability that a randomly chosen positive scores higher than a randomly chosen negative, i.e., (number of correctly-ordered positive/negative pairs + 0.5 · number of ties) / (n_pos · n_neg). Rather than the O(P·N) double loop over all pairs, I rank all scores together (sort once), then AUC = (sum of ranks of positives − n_pos·(n_pos+1)/2) / (n_pos · n_neg). That is O(n log n) from the sort. Steps: (1) pair each score with its label; (2) sort by score ascending and assign ranks 1..n; (3) crucially, handle ties by assigning the average rank to all tied elements (otherwise ties bias the result) — this is the U-statistic tie correction; (4) sum the ranks of the positive class and apply the formula. Edge cases to name: if n_pos or n_neg is zero, AUC is undefined (return NaN or raise); all-same scores gives AUC = 0.5 via the tie handling; and I must average ranks over tied groups, not assign them sequentially. I would validate against a small hand-computed example (e.g., scores [0.1,0.4,0.35,0.8] with labels [0,0,1,1] gives AUC 0.75). The threshold-sweep TPR/FPR trapezoidal method also works and is O(n log n) too, but the rank-sum form is shorter and handles ties cleanly.',
