    answer: 'The naive form is BCE = −[y·log(p) + (1−y)·log(1−p)], where p = sigmoid(z). This blows up when p is exactly 0 or 1: log(0) = −inf, and the intermediate sigmoid overflows for large |z| (exp of a big positive number). Two fixes. (1) If you already have probabilities p, clip them into [ε, 1−ε] (ε ~ 1e-7) before the log so you never take log(0) — a pragmatic guard. (2) The proper fix is to compute loss from the logits z directly, never materializing an unstable p: the stable identity is BCE(z, y) = max(z, 0) − z·y + log(1 + exp(−|z|)). The max(z,0) − z·y term and the log1p(exp(−|z|)) with the absolute value keep every exponent non-positive, so nothing overflows for large |z| in either direction — this is exactly what framework "with_logits" losses do. The gradient is beautifully simple in logit space: ∂BCE/∂z = sigmoid(z) − y (averaged over the batch), which is the well-known "prediction minus target" form — I would use a stable sigmoid, sigmoid(z) = where(z>=0, 1/(1+exp(−z)), exp(z)/(1+exp(z))), to avoid overflow there too. Edge cases: batch mean vs. sum reduction, label values must be 0/1 (or in [0,1] for soft labels, which the formula still handles), and empty batch. The headline point: operate in logit space and use log1p / the |z| trick rather than clipping after the fact.',
